1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,568
The saved Week 16 plan calls for review of PR01–PR33, selected
checklist and measurement readings, targeted remediation and a Mock
OSCE with extensive documentation and formal handover. The saved
Moodle Weekend 16 section has no lesson body or rubric.

2
00:00:20,568 --> 00:00:34,752
This lesson supplies an original study structure for that plan. It is
not AET courseware, a school checklist, a clinical sign-off, or
permission to perform a procedure outside your certification and
current service direction.

3
00:00:34,752 --> 00:00:52,824
Keep the current BC Emergency Health Services procedure page open
while reviewing. Record its identifier and revision date, then work
through purpose, indication, prerequisites, authorization, sequence,
stop criteria, expected response and documentation.

4
00:00:52,824 --> 00:01:06,096
Do not turn a title or a checklist item into a remembered technique.
If the live guide and an older compilation differ, use the current
service source and ask the instructor to resolve the school-copy
mismatch.

5
00:01:06,096 --> 00:01:26,760
Use the assigned glucose and cardiac-assessment readings to practise
the reason behind a skill. A measurement is justified by the clinical
question, not by the fact that it appears on a checklist. Explain what
the result can and cannot tell you, what safety checks precede it and
which actual authorized pathway guides the response.

6
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:50,280
The complete CPSC Appendix Two index contains eighty-nine entries, not
eighty-nine fully taught procedure bodies. The CNS Appendix Two
pointer remains unresolved and is not repaired by renaming the CPSC
index. In a school drill, identify which real checklist you are using
and do not improvise an unfamiliar step from a related title.

7
00:01:50,280 --> 00:02:13,008
Use the twelve VSM flashcards as retrieval prompts. Have a peer
observe the technique before revealing the card, then compare your
sequence and rationale with the source. A confident number is not
evidence of a reliable measurement. Include the device fit, relevant
positioning, signal quality and the reason to repeat or use another
authorized method.

8
00:02:13,008 --> 00:02:34,512
Make the difficulty specific: for example, the learner failed to
recognize a poor fit, not simply failed vital signs. This is an
original practice example, not a claim about your Appendix G. Repeat
with one controlled variation, record the observed change and connect
it to the patient consequence before increasing case complexity.

9
00:02:34,512 --> 00:02:50,616
The current PR set includes procedures that may be restricted to
advanced providers or governed by separate requirements. A Week 16
review assignment does not mean every listed skill is in a PCP’s scope
or must be performed during the school Mock OSCE.

10
00:02:50,616 --> 00:03:18,360
The previously missing Pinel device teaching now uses actual
manufacturer manuals together with the current service restraint
policies. This is not recovery of the historical PR25 body. PR30
remains a resource collection with its actual checklist and pediatric
method; a volumetric label returns a syringe manual. Learn the
supplied bodies and preserve their limits instead of inventing a
different algorithm.

11
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:35,640
The school plan links Week 16 remediation to learner-specific gaps.
Start with a behavior that someone actually observed, not a vague
label such as “be more confident.” Example: “I gave a handover but
omitted the response to the last intervention.”

12
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:47,880
The official Appendix G was not in the saved source set. This original
gap-to-drill card helps prepare; it is not Appendix G and should not
replace or be submitted instead of the school form.

13
00:03:47,880 --> 00:04:02,592
Choose a visible behavior and a reliable observer cue. For example,
the observer can note whether you identified an unresolved risk,
communicated it to the receiving clinician, and documented the
patient’s response with a time reference.

14
00:04:02,592 --> 00:04:16,080
Do not invent a pass score or a critical-fail rule. The BC Provincial
Examination Guidelines describe the provincial licensing examination;
they do not establish AET’s internal Mock OSCE scoring.

15
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:38,904
If a learner can recite a pressure technique but ignores an unreliable
result in a case, the gap is transfer, not merely recall. Pair the
relevant A&P or skills route with a short case cue, then observe
whether the learner recognizes uncertainty and changes the plan
appropriately. A second correct recitation alone does not answer that
question.

16
00:04:38,904 --> 00:05:01,824
Use the MSN evaluation loop to compare the intended learning behaviour
with actual evidence. If the behaviour does not improve, examine the
cue, instruction, equipment and feedback before merely adding
repetitions. Save the next narrow target for supervised practice; do
not turn an original coaching card into a completed official school
form.

17
00:05:01,824 --> 00:05:26,832
The completed BC Provincial Examination Guidelines reading
distinguishes the school’s saved twenty-twenty-five copy, its literal
assigned pages, the body reached by the named grading topic and the
later twenty-twenty-six comparison. Provincial scoring and official-
result review are licensing processes. They do not automatically
define an AET practice station or a school appeal procedure.

18
00:05:26,832 --> 00:05:46,464
For the Mock OSCE, follow the instructor’s actual station instructions
and feedback. The available school plan specifies extensive
documentation and formal handover but supplies no numeric rubric. Ask
the instructor to clarify any pass or fail criterion before treating
it as official.

19
00:05:46,464 --> 00:06:00,432
A useful teach-back is short, specific and traceable to a source. If
you cannot identify the current page, role boundary or expected
reassessment, reopen the material rather than filling the gap from
memory.

20
00:06:00,432 --> 00:06:12,000
After the school station, separate what you observed, what the
instructor said and what you infer. Keep the next drill narrow enough
that one behavior can be practised and measured.
